# Routing Parameters and Route Definition Mismatches in Laravel

You are encountering a classic routing issue where the URL structure matches your expectation, but the server cannot find a corresponding route definition to handle the request. When you hover over the link `{{ url('projects/display', $project->id) }}`, Laravel correctly generates `/projects/display/2`. However, clicking it triggers a 404 error because the router does not recognize this specific path pattern as a valid endpoint.

The root cause lies in the discrepancy between your custom route definition and how Laravel's routing engine processes parameters. In your `routes/web.php` file, you have defined the route explicitly:

```php
Route::get('/projects/display', 'ProjectsController@getDisplay');
```

This specific line tells Laravel to expect a request at exactly `/projects/display`. It has no provision for accepting additional arguments like an ID. When the browser sends a request to `/projects/display/2`, the router sees that this string does not match `/projects/display`. Since there is no wildcard pattern defined here, Laravel throws a 404 Not Found error before your controller method `getDisplay` is ever invoked.

To fix this, you need to define the route with a parameter placeholder. In Laravel, parameters are enclosed in curly braces `{}`. You must update your route definition to accept the ID:

```php
Route::get('/projects/display/{id}', 'ProjectsController@getDisplay');
```

By adding `{id}`, you instruct the router to capture whatever number follows `/projects/display/` and pass it as a variable to the controller. Laravel will then automatically resolve this value into the first parameter of your method signature.

Consequently, your `ProjectsController` method needs to be adjusted to accept this argument. Your current implementation looks like this:

```php
public function getDisplay($id) {
    // This part is actually correct for receiving the ID
}
```

While the method signature `getDisplay($id)` is technically capable of receiving the parameter, there is a logic error within the method itself that will cause issues once the routing is fixed. Currently, you are retrieving all projects:

```php
$project = Project::all(); 
return view('projects.display')->withProject($project);
```

If this code executes, your view will attempt to display a collection of all projects rather than the single project corresponding to the `$id`. You must use the passed argument to fetch the specific record. The corrected logic should look like this:

```php
public function getDisplay($id) {
    // Find the specific project using the ID from the route parameter
    $project = Project::findOrFail($id);

    // If you prefer a softer error (returning 404 handled by framework), use findOrFail
    // $project = Project::find($id); 

    return view('projects.display')->with('project', $project);
}
```

Note the change to `withProject` in your original code versus `with` in the corrected version. While Laravel allows dynamic property injection like `$view->withProject()`, it is often cleaner and more explicit to use standard naming conventions like `$view->with('project', $project)` or ensure the method name matches exactly if you strictly want to use custom helpers. More importantly, using `findOrFail` ensures that if someone tries to access `/projects/display/999` where no project exists, Laravel will automatically return a 404 page rather than passing `null` to your view and potentially causing an error in the blade template when trying to access properties of a non-existent model.

### Why This Approach Works

Using route parameters like `{id}` is the standard Laravel pattern for RESTful resource management. It decouples the URL structure from the internal logic, allowing you to handle dynamic content without hardcoding IDs into your routes or views.

If you prefer a more concise approach, Laravel also supports named routes which can be referenced directly in your view:

```php
Route::resource('projects', 'ProjectsController');
```

This single line registers standard RESTful routes including `GET /projects/{id}` mapped to the `show` method by default. However, since you have a custom display logic in `getDisplay`, keeping the explicit route definition with `{id}` is the correct path forward for your specific requirements. This ensures type safety and clear separation of concerns between your URL structure and your controller actions.
